-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Building ATHENA
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA is built with CMake. The inherited GNU autotools files are not maintained
for ATHENA.

The primary supported development path is Linux with Qt 6. Native KDE/Wayland
is the most tested desktop configuration.

ATHENA also has scripted packaging paths for redistributable Linux AppImages and
an experimental native Windows x86_64 cross build. Those paths are intentionally
separate from the ordinary local development build.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dependencies
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA intentionally assumes a modern desktop development environment. The
CMake configuration currently requires, among others:

  CMake, a C/C++ compiler, pkg-config, Qt 6, Guile 1.8, Boost headers,
  mimalloc, SQLite3, Python 3, uv, FreeType, PNG, JPEG, zlib, zstd,
  ImageMagick/MagickWand, Iconv, GnuTLS, GMP, Ghostscript, resvg with
  ResvgQt.h, libtcc, and patchelf.

On openSUSE, this is a useful zypper baseline for the ordinary system
dependencies:

  sudo zypper install \
    git cmake ninja pkgconf-pkg-config patchelf \
    qt6-base-devel qt6-svg-devel qt6-wayland-devel qt6-qt5compat-devel \
    kf6-kio-devel kf6-kconfigwidgets-devel \
    boost-devel \
    mimalloc-devel sqlite3-devel \
    python3 python313-uv \
    freetype2-devel libpng16-devel libjpeg8-devel zlib-devel \
    libzstd-devel ImageMagick-devel \
    libgnutls-devel gmp-devel ghostscript resvg-devel

Package names vary by distribution and repository. If CMake reports that
ResvgQt.h or libtcc is missing, install the corresponding development package
from your distribution or a local package/source build. ATHENA uses libtcc for
namespace sorter support.

ATHENA no longer uses Graphviz for namespace hierarchy graph layout. Reverse,
direct, global, and export hierarchy diagrams are laid out through Boost graph
algorithms in the application.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Guile 1.8
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA currently needs Guile 1.8. Do not rely on a modern distribution's default
Guile development package unless it actually provides Guile 1.8 pkg-config
metadata.

The Guile 1.8 package used for the verified build is:

  https://build.opensuse.org/package/show/home:jmnotin:TeXmacs/Guile18

Build and install Guile 1.8 separately before configuring ATHENA. If the package
is installed system-wide and pkg-config can already find guile-1.8, no extra
environment variable is needed. If you install it under a private prefix, for
example $HOME/opt/guile18, export its pkg-config path before running CMake:

  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/opt/guile18/lib64/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH"

Verify that pkg-config sees the correct version:

  pkg-config --modversion guile-1.8

The verified build used Guile 1.8.8.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DataArt Python environment
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA's DataArt PDF cover generator is a uv-managed Python project in
ATHENA/tools/data-art. CMake synchronizes its locked Python environment during
the build by running:

  uv sync --locked --no-dev --project ATHENA/tools/data-art

The generated ATHENA/tools/data-art/.venv directory is build/runtime state. Do
not commit it to source control. For a binary archive, either include a deliberate
offline runtime bundle or require the packager to run the CMake build once so uv
can synchronize the environment.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Recommended compiler: Intel oneAPI ICPX
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ATHENA strongly recommends Intel oneAPI C++ (icpx). The project is large, and
icpx has been the preferred compiler during current ATHENA development.

Example oneAPI setup on an RPM/zypper based system:

  sudo rpm --import \
    https://yum.repos.intel.com/oneapi/GPG-PUB-KEY-INTEL-SW-PRODUCTS.PUB
  sudo zypper addrepo -f https://yum.repos.intel.com/oneapi oneAPI
  sudo zypper refresh
  sudo zypper install intel-oneapi-compiler-dpcpp-cpp

Before configuring ATHENA, load the oneAPI environment:

  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  which icx
  which icpx

If the build directory was previously configured with another compiler, remove
it before switching compilers.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Configure and build
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

From the repository root:

  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/opt/guile18/lib64/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH"

  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_qt6 \
    -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug

  python3 patch_ads.py build_qt6/_deps/ads-src
  CCACHE_DISABLE=1 cmake --build build_qt6 -j "$(nproc)"

Using all available processor cores is recommended; `nproc` is the preferred
way to pick the parallelism level.

Pass `-G Ninja` if you prefer Ninja. The ADS patch script is the source of
truth for ATHENA's Qt Advanced Docking System changes, including Qt6/Wayland
native toplevel drag and single-tab floating pane behavior.

CMake patches the built executable with:

  RUNPATH=$ORIGIN/../lib

This lets a copied ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin resolve bundled libraries from
ATHENA/lib without manually running patchelf.

For a local release-mode binary, use a separate build directory:

  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/opt/guile18/lib64/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH"

  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_rel \
    -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo

  python3 patch_ads.py build_rel/_deps/ads-src
  CCACHE_DISABLE=1 cmake --build build_rel -j "$(nproc)"

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Run from the source runtime tree
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For ATHENA development, do not run the executable directly from build_qt6/src.
Copy the build output into the ATHENA runtime tree first:

  cp -f build_qt6/src/ATHENA.bin ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin
  mkdir -p ATHENA/lib
  cp -a build_qt6/x64/lib/libqt6advanceddocking*.so* ATHENA/lib/

Then run:

  ./ATHENA/StartATHENA.sh

This matters because ATHENA expects its Scheme code, packages, tools, assets,
and runtime paths to be arranged under the ATHENA tree.

`StartATHENA.sh`, `StartATHENA.qt6.sh`, and `StartATHENA.qt6.wayland.sh`
currently use native Wayland. They clear Qt scale override variables, avoid
forcing fcitx input modules, prepend `ATHENA/lib` to `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, and run:

  ./bin/ATHENA.bin --platform wayland

For XCB experiments, run the binary manually with the platform and input-method
environment you want to test. XCB is no longer the default runtime path.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Assemble a binary release tree
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The complete release workflow is scripted and idempotent:

  tools/release/build-all.sh

It configures and builds the native Linux release, assembles the tarball, runs
the container AppImage/DEB/openSUSE RPM/RHEL RPM workflow, cross-builds the
Windows runtime, smoke-tests it with Wine, creates the ZIP, and writes SHA-256
files. It only creates local artifacts; it does not tag, upload, or publish a
GitHub release.

Useful options include:

  tools/release/build-all.sh --dry-run
  tools/release/build-all.sh --skip-windows
  tools/release/build-all.sh --skip-container
  tools/release/build-all.sh --cmake-arg=-DUSE_KF6_KIO_FILE_DIALOGS=OFF

For a native Linux-only build:

  tools/release/build-all.sh --skip-container --skip-windows

All release runtime trees must be assembled through
`tools/release/runtime_policy.py`. It keeps runtime code and documentation but
excludes model weights (`.gguf`, `.safetensors`, `.onnx`, `.pt`, `.pth`,
`.ckpt`, and model `.bin` files), Python virtual environments and generated
caches. Formula cleaner, embedding, and artifact classification models are
optional local resources and are never included directly in the main ATHENA
release packages.

Useful checks:

  readelf -d release/ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin | \
    grep -E 'RUNPATH|RPATH|qt6advanced'
  readelf -d release/ATHENA/lib/libqt6advanceddockingd.so.4.3.1 | \
    grep -E 'RUNPATH|RPATH|SONAME'
  ldd release/ATHENA/bin/ATHENA.bin | grep qt6advanceddocking

The ADS library should resolve from release/ATHENA/lib. The executable should
have RUNPATH $ORIGIN/../lib. The ADS library itself should not carry an
absolute build rpath.

Run the release tree with:

  ./release/ATHENA/StartATHENA.sh

Do not construct a release tree with a raw `cp` or `rsync` of `ATHENA/`.
`runtime_policy.py verify PATH` can be used to audit a staged runtime.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Container AppImage release builds
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For redistributable Linux bundles, use the scripted container workflow under
`tools/container-build`:

  tools/container-build/build-athena-container.sh

The workflow builds in an openSUSE Leap 15.6 container, uses Qt 6, builds Guile
1.8 from a source tree, prefers Intel `icx`/`icpx` when available, and produces:

  container_build/ATHENA-dev/
  container_build/ATHENA-rel/
  container_build/ATHENA-dev.AppImage
  container_build/ATHENA-rel.AppImage
  container_build/packages/ATHENA-<version>-dev-linux-x86_64.deb
  container_build/packages/ATHENA-<version>-rel-linux-x86_64.deb
  container_build/packages/ATHENA-<version>-<flavor>-opensuse-x86_64.rpm
  container_build/packages/ATHENA-<version>-<flavor>-rhel-x86_64.rpm

It can also validate the AppImages in an Ubuntu container:

  tools/container-build/validate-appimages-ubuntu.sh

Native package metadata and payload structure are validated without installing
the RPMs or DEBs on the host:

  tools/container-build/validate-native-packages.sh

Useful knobs:

  ATHENA_GUILE18_SOURCE=/path/to/guile-1.8.8
  ATHENA_BUILD_JOBS=16
  ATHENA_SKIP_UBUNTU_VALIDATE=1
  ATHENA_ALLOW_SYSTEM_COMPILER=1

`ATHENA_GUILE18_SOURCE` must point to a Guile 1.8 source tree. Do not replace
Guile 1.8 with Guile 2 or Guile 3 for ATHENA.

The AppImage launcher defaults to Wayland and follows the same scale/input
policy as `StartATHENA.qt6.wayland.sh`.

The container runtime copy and both native package builders enforce the same
model-free runtime policy as the native tarball.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Windows x86_64 cross build
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Native Windows support is experimental and is built from Linux through
MinGW-w64. Use the dedicated helper tree:

  tools/windows-deps/build-athena-win64-release.sh

The script uses a Windows target prefix under `build_windows/prefix`, configures
an isolated `build_windows` tree, and assembles:

  release/windows/ATHENA

The all-platform release driver additionally creates the canonical archive:

  release/ATHENA-<version>-windows-x86_64.zip

It runs a Wine version smoke test by default. Use `--skip-wine-test` only when
Wine cannot be installed on the packaging host.

The Windows build must use target libraries from the Windows prefix, including
Qt6, Guile 1.8.8, mimalloc, ADS, libtcc, resvg, llama.cpp, and the other
runtime DLLs. It must not consume Linux host Qt or host pkg-config metadata.
See `tools/windows-deps/README.md` for dependency preparation details.

The CMake Windows configuration distinguishes 64-bit MinGW with `OS_MINGW64`
and uses the `src/Subsystems/Windows64` system layer. This is required for the
Windows LLP64 ABI and Guile smob pointer/tag handling.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Install
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For a system install, use the usual CMake install workflow:

  cmake --install build_qt6

Set a custom prefix at configure time if needed:

  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/opt/guile18/lib64/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH"
  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_qt6 \
    -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/athena

The primary tested workflow is the ATHENA runtime tree and release packaging
described above; use CMake install only if you intentionally want a system-style
installation.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Troubleshooting
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If CMake cannot find a dependency, install the corresponding development package
and rerun the configure step. If CMake cannot find Guile 1.8 specifically, check
PKG_CONFIG_PATH and verify:

  pkg-config --modversion guile-1.8

If generated files or Scheme cache entries interfere with a local development
build, remove the build directory and ATHENA cache, then configure again:

  rm -rf build_qt6
  rm -rf ~/.ATHENA/system/cache
  source /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh
  export PKG_CONFIG_PATH="$HOME/opt/guile18/lib64/pkgconfig:$PKG_CONFIG_PATH"
  CC=icx CXX=icpx cmake -S . -B build_qt6 \
    -DATHENA_GUI=Qt6 \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
